WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.860
Hi, how are you?
Cheers, Willie.

2
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.905
Look at your studio, I am so jealous.

3
00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.738
Really?

4
00:00:06.738 --> 00:00:07.571
Oh my gosh.

5
00:00:07.571 --> 00:00:08.550
I also wanna congratulate you

6
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.070
on another season of the show.

7
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.060
Thank God, I'm so relieved, Willie.

8
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:15.810
I mean, People don't realize how hard it is.

9
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.810
How hard it is to start something, anything.

10
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.490 line:15%
To start something during a pandemic

11
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.040 line:15%
without the plan that you had sketched out for a year,

12
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.339 line:15%
all of a sudden, it's totally different.

13
00:00:24.339 --> 00:00:25.172 line:15%
Yep.

14
00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:26.400
And to succeed the way you've succeeded

15
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.950
is a huge tribute to you and your crew, so congrats.

16
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.830
Thank you, it's all about the crew.

17
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:31.663
(crew cheer)

18
00:00:31.663 --> 00:00:33.420
It's a big deal.
We're all we've had.

19
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.510
And so it's a testament to us.

20
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.840
And I am saying this to the man who truly knows

21
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.580
what making a show is all about.

22
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.130
And you just celebrated,

23
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.080
I see all your family and friends

24
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.102
celebrating your fifth anniversary of "Sunday TODAY."

25
00:00:50.102 --> 00:00:51.437
Thank you, yeah.

26
00:00:51.437 --> 00:00:54.830
Yeah, like you, we started it five years ago

27
00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.870
not quite knowing where it was heading.

28
00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.450
Was doing something a little bit different

29
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.440
within "the TODAY Show" family.

30
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.360
and, you know, we tinkered with it along the way,

31
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.920
the same way you have probably.

32
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.070
And I think we finally landed on something

33
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.200
that might stick around for awhile.

34
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.640
It's interesting when you are so kind of gut convinced

35
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.570
that something's gonna work and it just doesn't.

36
00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:14.780
Totally, totally.

37
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.860
When I started that show.

38
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.040
So my other job, I was on "The TODAY Show" at the time,

39
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.280
hosting the third hour, and "Morning Joe" on MSNBC.

40
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.590
So I had these two jobs.

41
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.640
And my plan was, what if I smashed those two shows together

42
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.160
and make some combination of that.

43
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.540
And it was a little too much of the conversation off the top

44
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.800
at the beginning, where the audience needed to actually know

45
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.840
what was going on in the world

46
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.480
and not launch into the conversation

47
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.770
the way we do on "Morning Joe."

48
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.370
So that was part of the learning.

49
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.920
So we walked that back a little bit.

50
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.610
We still have our conversations, of course,

51
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.130
but by and large, it's, here's the news,

52
00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.100
and then we make the turn

53
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.010
to it being kind of a magazine show.

54
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.130
Well, we're gonna do thank you

55
00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.410
for doing the "Art of the Interview"

56
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.860
which is a digital series I do to pick the minds of...

57
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.230
people that I admire,

58
00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:06.110
especially in the journalistic sense, curious people,

59
00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.773
people who have figured out how to have an art

60
00:02:08.773 --> 00:02:11.500
in the interview itself.

61
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.460
What is something that is advice,

62
00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.268
I'm asking for myself here.

63
00:02:18.268 --> 00:02:19.101
(Willie laughs)

64
00:02:19.101 --> 00:02:23.690
What is advice to asking that surprising question

65
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.250
that might catch off guard, might be a bit pushy

66
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.800
but really is gonna ultimately bring insight

67
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.680
into that person we're not getting otherwise.

68
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.700
I think it starts with trust.

69
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.670
And I think you're really good at that actually,

70
00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.520
which is that you are open yourself.

71
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.230 line:15%
You're willing to share,

72
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.499 line:15%
which you've been on our show twice, by the way.

73
00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.190 line:15%
I'm so lucky.
And it's a short list.

74
00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.960
It's you, Dolly, Ina Garten.

75
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.742
There's like four people and you're in that group.

76
00:02:52.742 --> 00:02:54.477
What company, my goodness.

77
00:02:54.477 --> 00:02:58.010 line:15%
But you, I know if I can build trust with you

78
00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.600 line:15%
and you know that number one, I'm well-researched,

79
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.500
that I'm not interested in whatever the gotcha question is

80
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.330
from the gossip magazine, that's just not what I do.

81
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:08.860
If you can start a conversation,

82
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.570
and frankly on our show, we have time.

83
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.570
I sit with somebody, as you know, for 45 minutes or an hour.

84
00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.170
So even if I don't know the person, I'm building a rapport.

85
00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.579
So once you have that trust,

86
00:03:18.579 --> 00:03:20.810
you might not even have to ask the question.

87
00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.570
They might just start sharing something with you

88
00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:23.700
or talking about,

89
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.990
you know, I don't like to zero in on pain in someone's life

90
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.780
but if I feel like we've got enough of a relationship

91
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.380
in the interview that they're willing to go there,

92
00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.210
then I will, or a lot of times, as I say,

93
00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.920
it will just come out because we've built that rapport

94
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.050
over the course of the interview.

95
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:44.050
I definitely am someone who is willing to go there myself.

96
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.230
And I would like to welcome anyone else to go there with me.

97
00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.270
And what you just said made me think of your signature piece

98
00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.890
in your show, the highs and the lows.

99
00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.310
And I was wondering if I could turn that question on to you.

100
00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.100
Yeah, I would love that.

101
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.730
And why don't we start with the lows

102
00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:01.830
so we can end on the highs.

103
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.540
Oh, okay, all right.

104
00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.880
I guess professionally, as I think about it,

105
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.290
it's interesting, we've been celebrating

106
00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:09.260
five years of the show

107
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.380
and it's all been sort of the glossy interviews

108
00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.420
and all the great people we've talked to.

109
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.370
But the lows are, as I said, it's still a news show.

110
00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:18.560
So a lot of times you'll wake up on a Sunday morning,

111
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.550
unfortunately, and something terrible has happened

112
00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:21.650
the night before.

113
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:24.660
And so in our first year of the show, it's June of 2016,

114
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.710
We're only two months into it.

115
00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.870
And it was the Pulse nightclub shooting in Orlando.

116
00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.080
And I was on the air that morning

117
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.630
I think for seven and a half hours alone, no commercials,

118
00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.850
just talking and trying to get everybody up to speed

119
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.330
and interviewing people.

120
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:40.760
And I'll never forget the moment

121
00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.510
when the police first came out to the microphones

122
00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.250
and we're trying to assess what exactly had happened.

123
00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:49.230
And he said, "We have 50 casualties."

124
00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.897
and I'll never forget saying to my producer off-mic,

125
00:04:51.897 --> 00:04:53.950
"Did he say 50?

126
00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.140
Did he mean five?

127
00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:55.973
Did he say 15?"

128
00:04:55.973 --> 00:04:56.806
Which is bad enough.

129
00:04:56.806 --> 00:04:57.639
"Did he say 50?"

130
00:04:57.639 --> 00:04:59.930
And they said, "Yeah, he said 50."

131
00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.440
And then over the course of that morning,

132
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.580
I'm interviewing people who've just lived through

133
00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.520
the worst thing you could ever live through.

134
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.730
I can't sort of express to people enough,

135
00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.980
what type of person

136
00:05:13.468 --> 00:05:16.860
with an empathic heart, a curious mind

137
00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.470
and someone who is, again, incredibly well versed

138
00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.390
in the ways of the world, the current news,

139
00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.190
and humanity in general,

140
00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.590
to be able to go on the air for seven hours,

141
00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.900
hear these stories, tell these stories.

142
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.680
I think you can really feel the authenticity of someone.

143
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:36.513
I agree.

144
00:05:36.513 --> 00:05:39.270
All I've done for my whole career is just be myself,

145
00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.510
just totally be myself.

146
00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.950
I mean, you can't play TV or try to be the anchorman

147
00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.210
or what would Walter Cronkite do right now?

148
00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.480
You just have to be you.

149
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.670
Okay, the highs, well, let's end on the highs

150
00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.050
before we go to commercial break.

151
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:53.060
Well, for me, it's the people you don't hear from a lot,

152
00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.790
when we land an interview with someone like that.

153
00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:58.940
So Al Pacino was one of them last year.

154
00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.060
I mean, look at this picture

155
00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.420
where I am cruising around Beverly Hills

156
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:07.240
in a drop top Cadillac Eldorado with Al Pacino.

157
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.250
And that is an out of body experience.

158
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:10.593
Yeah, no, that's a moment.

159
00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.420
I sat down with him,

160
00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.410
he doesn't do a lot of interviews, as I said,

161
00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.290
for about an hour.

162
00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.090
We talked through his career.

163
00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:18.580
And he said he was turning 80,

164
00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.350
and he felt like it was time and he was ready to talk.

165
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.870
And then he just agreed to everything.

166
00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:23.980
Yeah, we can do an hour,

167
00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.130
and then we'll go for a drive around Beverly Hills.

168
00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:26.963
And all these people are yelling at him

169
00:06:26.963 --> 00:06:29.030
and he's having the time of his life.

170
00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.410
And I thought, what life is this, you know?

171
00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:35.790
How did I end up in this car with Al Pacino riding shotgun.

172
00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:40.190
Homework, curiosity, and being a good person, you know?

173
00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:42.910
I mean, if you use the ladder of other people,

174
00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.040
It just won't be there.

175
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.600
And if you use the ladder of yourself, you will build slowly

176
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.578
but there's a strong foundation there.

177
00:06:49.578 --> 00:06:52.745 line:15%
(upbeat bright music)

